msgid "<0/> can also say that something might happen. This sentence can mean he or she is allowed to come tomorrow, or might: <1/>"
msgstr "<0/> can also say that something might happen. This sentence can mean he or she is allowed to come tomorrow, or might: <1/>"

msgid "<0/> is about being allowed. With <1>ei</1> it says mustn’t: <2/> In a question it asks whether you may, as <3/> does too: <4/> Tap one to hear its sentence."
msgstr "<0/> is about being allowed. With <1>ei</1> it says mustn’t: <2/> In a question it asks whether you may, as <3/> does too: <4/> Tap one to hear its sentence."

msgid "<0/> is for what you learned. To say you know how to swim: <1/> <2/> usually says nothing stops you: <3/>"
msgstr "<0/> is for what you learned. To say you know how to swim: <1/> <2/> usually says nothing stops you: <3/>"

msgid "<0/> is I have to, and it takes the <1>-ma</1> infinitive from lesson {infinitives}: <2/>"
msgstr "<0/> is I have to, and it takes the <1>-ma</1> infinitive from lesson {infinitives}: <2/>"

msgid "<0/> is must, but <1>ei pea</1> is don’t have to: <2/> For mustn’t, Estonian usually switches to <3>ei tohi</3>: <4/> <5/>, no need, means the same as <6>ei pea</6>. Tap one to hear it said about swimming."
msgstr "<0/> is must, but <1>ei pea</1> is don’t have to: <2/> For mustn’t, Estonian usually switches to <3>ei tohi</3>: <4/> <5/>, no need, means the same as <6>ei pea</6>. Tap one to hear it said about swimming."

msgid "<0/> says something is allowed, and you also offer with it: <1/> To let someone do something: <2/>"
msgstr "<0/> says something is allowed, and you also offer with it: <1/> To let someone do something: <2/>"

msgid "<0/> with the <1>-da</1> infinitive says something has to be done, without naming who: <2/> Put a person in the <3>-l</3> form from lesson {having} to make it theirs: <4/>"
msgstr "<0/> with the <1>-da</1> infinitive says something has to be done, without naming who: <2/> Put a person in the <3>-l</3> form from lesson {having} to make it theirs: <4/>"

msgid "<0/>, I need, takes the <1>-da</1> infinitive of a verb: <2/> Tap a form to hear its sentence."
msgstr "<0/>, I need, takes the <1>-da</1> infinitive of a verb: <2/> Tap a form to hear its sentence."

msgid "<0>Ei oska</0> says you never learned. You learned as a child."
msgstr "<0>Ei oska</0> says you never learned. You learned as a child."

msgid "<0>Ei pea</0> is don’t have to, and <1>ei tohi</1> is mustn’t: <2>Sa ei pea tulema. Sa ei tohi tulla.</2>"
msgstr "<0>Ei pea</0> is don’t have to, and <1>ei tohi</1> is mustn’t: <2>Sa ei pea tulema. Sa ei tohi tulla.</2>"

msgid "<0>Ei pea</0> says you don’t have to work. You do work tomorrow."
msgstr "<0>Ei pea</0> says you don’t have to work. You do work tomorrow."

msgid "<0>Ei saa</0> says something stops you. She asked whether you know how."
msgstr "<0>Ei saa</0> says something stops you. She asked whether you know how."

msgid "<0>Ei tohi</0> says you’re not allowed to swim. Nothing stops you today."
msgstr "<0>Ei tohi</0> says you’re not allowed to swim. Nothing stops you today."

msgid "<0>Oskan</0> is for what you learned: <1>Ma oskan ujuda.</1> <2>Saan</2> usually says nothing stops you: <3>Ma saan homme tulla.</3>"
msgstr "<0>Oskan</0> is for what you learned: <1>Ma oskan ujuda.</1> <2>Saan</2> usually says nothing stops you: <3>Ma saan homme tulla.</3>"

msgid "<0>Oskan</0>: you learned how."
msgstr "<0>Oskan</0>: you learned how."

msgid "<0>Pean</0> is I have to. <1>Tuleb</1> with the <2>-da</2> infinitive says it has to be done; on its own it names no one. <3>Mul on vaja</3> is I need to."
msgstr "<0>Pean</0> is I have to. <1>Tuleb</1> with the <2>-da</2> infinitive says it has to be done; on its own it names no one. <3>Mul on vaja</3> is I need to."

msgid "<0>Pean</0> says you have to come. Nobody makes you; you’re free."
msgstr "<0>Pean</0> says you have to come. Nobody makes you; you’re free."

msgid "<0>Saan</0>: usually, nothing stops you, not time, place or health."
msgstr "<0>Saan</0>: usually, nothing stops you, not time, place or health."

msgid "<0>Võin</0> and <1>tohin</1> both say it’s allowed; <2>võin</2> also offers: <3>Ma võin sind oodata.</3> <4>Tohin</4> is mostly heard with <5>ei</5>: <6>Siin ei tohi süüa.</6>"
msgstr "<0>Võin</0> and <1>tohin</1> both say it’s allowed; <2>võin</2> also offers: <3>Ma võin sind oodata.</3> <4>Tohin</4> is mostly heard with <5>ei</5>: <6>Siin ei tohi süüa.</6>"

msgid "<et>Oskan</et> is for what you know how to do; <et>saan</et> usually says nothing stops you. Both take the -da infinitive."
msgstr "<et>Oskan</et> is for what you know how to do; <et>saan</et> usually says nothing stops you. Both take the -da infinitive."

msgid "<et>Pean</et> and <et>ei pea</et> take the -ma infinitive; <et>mul on vaja</et>, and <et>tuleb</et> meaning has to, take the -da infinitive."
msgstr "<et>Pean</et> and <et>ei pea</et> take the -ma infinitive; <et>mul on vaja</et>, and <et>tuleb</et> meaning has to, take the -da infinitive."

msgid "<et>Võin</et> also offers: <et>Ma võin sind oodata.</et> It takes the -da infinitive."
msgstr "<et>Võin</et> also offers: <et>Ma võin sind oodata.</et> It takes the -da infinitive."

msgid "<et>Võin</et> and <et>tohin</et> say it’s allowed, and <et>ei tohi</et> says it isn’t. All take the -da infinitive."
msgstr "<et>Võin</et> and <et>tohin</et> say it’s allowed, and <et>ei tohi</et> says it isn’t. All take the -da infinitive."

